WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:01.610
Module 9.

00:01.960 --> 00:04.665
When it goes wrong: incident response.

00:05.015 --> 00:07.662
Errors will happen even in well-run firms.

00:08.012 --> 00:10.955
This module sets out the steps to take when an

00:10.955 --> 00:13.898
AI error is discovered, with the lesson from Mata v.

00:14.248 --> 00:18.231
Avianca about what happens when lawyers do not correct course.

00:19.981 --> 00:23.844
By the end of this module you will be able

00:23.844 --> 00:27.707
to: follow the six-step incident response; meet duties of candor

00:27.707 --> 00:31.184
and client communication; notify insurers and vendors on time.

00:32.934 --> 00:35.918
Start with the lesson from Mata versus Avianca.

00:36.268 --> 00:38.230
The fake citations were bad.

00:38.580 --> 00:41.671
But according to the court, what made it worse was

00:41.671 --> 00:44.761
that the lawyers continued to stand by the fake opinions

00:44.761 --> 00:47.543
after judicial orders had called their existence into question.

00:47.893 --> 00:52.533
Courts and regulators repeatedly distinguish between lawyers who make an error and

00:52.533 --> 00:56.786
correct it promptly, and lawyers who deny, delay or double down.

00:57.136 --> 00:59.613
Speed and candor are the most powerful tools

00:59.613 --> 01:01.780
you have once an error is discovered.

01:03.530 --> 01:05.550
Here is the six-step response.

01:05.900 --> 01:09.539
One, stop and contain: halt further use of the

01:09.539 --> 01:12.773
output and pull any pending filings or communications.

01:13.123 --> 01:17.265
Two, report internally to the AI Governance Lead, within two business

01:17.265 --> 01:21.030
days at most, and immediately if a deadline is live.

01:21.380 --> 01:24.985
Three, preserve evidence: put logs, versions and review records on

01:24.985 --> 01:28.589
legal hold and ask the vendor to preserve its logs.

01:28.939 --> 01:32.539
Four, correct the record: inform the client and, where the

01:32.539 --> 01:36.138
rules require, the court or tribunal, and fix the error.

01:36.488 --> 01:41.863
Five, notify your professional liability insurer, or insurers, promptly.

01:42.213 --> 01:46.027
Most policies have strict notice requirements, and reporting a circumstance

01:46.027 --> 01:49.841
early can protect cover for a claim that comes later.

01:50.191 --> 01:53.757
And six, learn: log the incident, identify the

01:53.757 --> 01:56.878
root cause, and update training and controls.

01:58.628 --> 02:00.997
Two notifications are easily forgotten.

02:01.346 --> 02:05.834
The vendor: check the contract for any incident-notice deadline and give written notice within

02:05.834 --> 02:10.321
it, keeping a copy of the terms that were in force at the time.

02:10.671 --> 02:15.026
The insurer: report early, even if no claim has yet been made,

02:15.026 --> 02:19.019
including what happened, the tool involved and how you found out.

02:19.369 --> 02:20.669
And one important don't.

02:21.019 --> 02:24.937
Do not admit liability, offer a payment or agree a settlement

02:24.937 --> 02:28.856
without your insurer's consent, because doing so can jeopardise your cover.

02:30.606 --> 02:35.679
Every incident, and every near-miss, goes into the firm's AI incident log.

02:36.029 --> 02:40.731
Capture when and how it was discovered, the matter and the document version, the

02:40.731 --> 02:45.432
tool used, what the error was and whether anyone relied on it, the actions

02:45.432 --> 02:49.462
taken, the notifications made with dates, and the root cause and fix.

02:49.812 --> 02:53.008
Near-misses matter as much as incidents, because they show where

02:53.008 --> 02:55.884
the controls are weak before a client is harmed.

02:57.634 --> 02:59.282
Let's recap the key points.

02:59.632 --> 03:02.628
Correct fast and candidly; never double down.

03:02.978 --> 03:05.973
Report to the Governance Lead within two business days.

03:06.323 --> 03:09.934
Preserve evidence and notify insurers and vendors on time.

03:10.284 --> 03:12.571
Log near-misses as well as incidents.

03:14.321 --> 03:15.343
Knowledge check.

03:15.693 --> 03:19.710
The day after filing, you find that one cited case does not exist.

03:20.060 --> 03:21.348
What comes first?

03:21.698 --> 03:25.193
Option A: Wait to see if opposing counsel notices.

03:25.543 --> 03:28.712
Option B: Contain, report to the Governance Lead, and

03:28.712 --> 03:31.882
move promptly to correct the filing with the court.

03:32.232 --> 03:36.516
Option C: Quietly file an amended brief without telling anyone.

03:36.866 --> 03:39.594
Take a moment to decide before the answer appears.

03:45.344 --> 03:46.993
The answer is option B.

03:47.343 --> 03:51.058
Prompt, candid correction through the firm's process is required, and

03:51.058 --> 03:54.773
it is the best protection for client, lawyer and firm.
